        growth_pct=17,
        summary="Bharti Airtel's FY26 results show real structural improvement -- net debt/EBITDA down to ~1.1x, capex intensity past its peak, FY26 operating FCF above Rs 41,500 crore, home broadband growing 31%+ YoY, and a 2.5-player market (Vi structurally propped up by government equity conversion, not competitively healthy) that keeps the industry aligned on tariff repair. But the 15.7% consolidated revenue headline is flattered by Indus Towers consolidation and Africa dollar-translation gains -- core India mobile revenue grew a more modest 7.7% YoY, ARPU rose only 4.9% YoY (lapping the last hike, not a new one), enterprise revenue was flat-to-down for the full year, and the next tariff hike (the market's central re-rating catalyst) has not yet been announced. At ~42-44x trailing earnings and ~10x book, the stock already prices in a lot of the good news building here. No balance-sheet or governance red flag, but full valuation and an unexecuted key catalyst argue for Hold, not for adding, and not for selling a fundamentally sound quality compounder into strength.",
        negative_para="The headline 15.7% consolidated revenue growth in Q4 FY26 is flattered by Indus Towers' full consolidation (~7% of group revenue) and Airtel Africa's dollar-translation gains; the India mobile business itself -- the piece most exposed to the domestic tariff-repair thesis -- grew a more modest 7.7% YoY. ARPU rose just 4.9% YoY to Rs 257, reflecting the lapping of the July-2024 tariff hike and organic premiumization rather than fresh pricing power; the next tariff hike (market chatter centres on ~15%, timing unconfirmed as of this review) has not been announced, leaving the market's central re-rating catalyst unexecuted while already substantially priced into the multiple. Enterprise (Airtel Business) revenue actually declined slightly for the full year (~Rs 22,094 crore to ~Rs 21,177 crore) even as home broadband boomed -- a segment the bull case typically treats as a growth engine has, in fact, been flat-to-down, with only a tentative 2.6% sequential improvement in the latest quarter. Earnings quality carries real noise: the effective tax rate has swung from a large credit (~-30% in Q4 FY25) to a modest positive rate (~8% in Q4 FY26), Africa's outsized FY26 profit jump (+147% YoY) is explicitly helped by forex/translation gains as the naira stabilised (a tailwind that need not repeat), and our internal PIT feed's headline net-profit figure for Q4 FY26 (Rs 9,247 crore) diverges materially from the company's own 'before exceptional items' figure (Rs 7,245 crore) -- a gap we could not fully decompose from public disclosures in this pass. At ~42-44x trailing consolidated earnings and ~10x book (against 20.5% ROE), valuation leaves little margin of safety if the next tariff hike is delayed, comes in smaller than the ~15% whispered, or draws political/regulatory pushback on affordability grounds. Ownership technicals add a mild overhang: Singtel (down to 27.5% from 31.4% in 2022) and a Mittal-linked entity (ICIL, ~1.8% sold across two 2025 block deals) have both been net sellers via block deals, framed as portfolio-recycling rather than a loss of conviction, but a recurring supply source nonetheless.",
        positive="Bharti Airtel's balance-sheet and cash-generation trajectory has genuinely inflected: net debt/EBITDA has fallen to ~1.1x (annualized) from 1.47x-1.98x a year earlier, FY26 India capex (ex-passive) moderated to ~Rs 31,000 crore as the 5G build-out matures, and operating free cash flow (EBITDA less capex) exceeded Rs 41,500 crore for FY26, with FY27 capex guided flat rather than higher -- sell-side models roughly Rs 1 lakh crore of cumulative FCF across FY26-27E. ARPU premiumization is working in relative terms: Airtel's Rs 257 ARPU sits meaningfully above Jio's ~Rs 214 even with a smaller (480.9m vs 500m+) subscriber base, evidence the company is genuinely capturing higher-value customers rather than just chasing volume. Home broadband is a real, fast-growing adjacency -- revenue up 31%+ YoY, 14.2 million home customers (+1.1 million in the quarter), 45 million fibre home-passes -- diversifying away from pure mobile. Competitive structure remains supportive: Vodafone Idea's survival now runs through continued government forbearance (Rs 36,950 crore of AGR dues converted to ~49% government equity, Rs 87,695 crore frozen until FY32) rather than competitive strength, meaning Airtel and Jio effectively face a 2.5-player market aligned on the direction of future tariff repair. Airtel Africa's FY26 revenue grew 24.0% in constant currency with profit up sharply to $813m, adding a genuinely growing, geographically diversified profit pool. No balance-sheet or governance red flag surfaced in this review (balance-sheet safety: GREEN, 0 quant red flags), and ROE/ROCE of 20.5%/27.4% are strong for the sector.",
        reverse_dcf="At ~Rs 1,936 (mid-July 2026) against trailing consolidated EPS of roughly Rs 46 (sum of the last four quarterly EPS prints in our PIT series), Bharti trades at ~42x trailing earnings and ~10x book on a 20.5% ROE -- among the richest multiples in the Nifty large-cap set (some web-sourced commentary cites 'north of 50x', which we believe uses a different, likely standalone or differently-timed EPS base; our own consolidated trailing-EPS calculation of ~42-44x is corroborated by the quant snapshot's PE of 43.9 and is the figure we underwrite). A multiple in that range, held flat, requires sustained EPS compounding in the high-teens-to-20s for several years to be earned back through growth rather than re-rating -- and importantly, a meaningful share of Bharti's recent PAT growth has come from items that will not repeat every year at the same magnitude: deferred-tax-rate swings, Africa forex/translation gains, and Indus Towers consolidation accounting effects, not purely organic ARPU-and-volume expansion. Our own bottom-up estimate of sustainable, 'clean' 3-year growth: India mobile ARPU-led growth of roughly 10-12% (premiumization plus one, still-unannounced tariff hike over the period) plus mid-single-digit subscriber growth, home broadband compounding off a small base at 25%+, enterprise recovering from its current flat/negative base to modest single-digit growth, and Africa growing mid-teens in constant-currency terms, blend to consolidated revenue/EBITDA growth in the mid-to-high teens; deleveraging (falling interest cost as net debt/EBITDA declines further from ~1.1x) then amplifies EBITDA growth into somewhat faster earnings-power growth. We land on ~17% p.a. as our genuine 3-year expected growth estimate -- a real, structurally-supported number for a 2.5-player market with a capex-past-peak FCF inflection, but one that only loosely clears what a persistent 42-44x multiple implicitly demands, and clears it mainly IF the next tariff hike lands roughly as the market currently whispers (~15%, timing and magnitude both genuinely uncertain). If that hike slips a year, or lands smaller/later on affordability-driven political pushback, the multiple has very little cushion and would likely compress toward the sell-side's own more conservative scenarios (bear-case targets cluster near Rs 1,300-1,550 versus a current price of ~Rs 1,936 and a consensus base case of Rs 1,900-2,100). Net judgment: fairly-to-fully valued against our own growth estimate, not cheap, and not a name to add to at this price -- but also not stretched enough on a business this structurally sound to force a Sell.",
