        growth_pct=30,
        summary="Eternal's headline 196% YoY consolidated revenue growth is substantially a Blinkit 1P-inventory revenue-recognition effect, not pure demand growth - real volume (Blinkit NOV +95.4% YoY, food delivery NOV +18.8% YoY) is still very strong but far more moderate than the headline. The decision-relevant fact is unit economics: Blinkit lost just Rs 3.02/order in FY26 versus Zepto's Rs 78.75 and Swiggy Instamart's Rs 85.18, a ~25x gap that is the best evidence its ~40-46% quick-commerce share is durable rather than subsidy-bought, and the build-out (Rs 2,600 cr invested in 2025-early 2026) has been funded without eroding Eternal's ~Rs 18,000 cr cash pile. Consolidated economics remain genuinely thin (FY26 PAT margin 0.67%, Q4 adjusted EBITDA margin ~2.4% of revenue), and at ~2.6-2.7x EV/run-rate NOV the market is pricing continued rapid growth and material margin expansion toward something like Meituan's historical trajectory - a real, not fantastical, but not risk-free ask, given Meituan's own 2025 margin collapse under a JD/Alibaba horizontal assault is the exact playbook Amazon Now and Flipkart Minutes could run in India. Hold: the structural moat widened this quarter, the balance sheet is not under stress, and Q1FY27 results (22 July 2026) are four days away - not a moment to sell an existing holding.",
        negative_para="Consolidated profitability is still genuinely thin, not just optically so because of trailing-PE mechanics - FY26 full-year PAT margin was just 0.67% (Rs 366 cr on Rs 54,364 cr revenue) and Q4FY26 consolidated adjusted EBITDA margin was only ~2.4% of adjusted revenue, so the quant screen's near-zero ROE/ROCE is a fair description of where the business actually is today, not solely a stale-data artifact. The headline revenue growth numbers (168.6% 1-year, 97.3% 3-year CAGR per the quant snapshot; 196% YoY in Q4) meaningfully overstate real demand growth because Blinkit's 1P inventory shift moved gross sale value onto the P&L instead of commission-only revenue - the true volume metric (Blinkit NOV) grew a still-strong-but-much-more-moderate 95.4% YoY, and this recognition-driven inflation needs to be understood before extrapolating the headline growth rate forward. At an estimated EV/run-rate-NOV of ~2.6-2.7x, the market is pricing sustained rapid growth plus material margin expansion toward a Meituan-like mature-market destination, and Meituan itself is the cautionary tale: its historically superior ~25% take-rate/4%-of-GMV profitability was overwhelmed in 2025 by a horizontal-platform subsidy war (JD.com and Alibaba), pushing it to an expected RMB 24 billion net loss - a structurally identical risk sits in India with Amazon Now and Flipkart Minutes each scaling past 500 dark stores (Flipkart adding ~100/month) and a contribution-positive JioMart already at ~800 stores. Blinkit's own store-count ramp (2,243 at Q4FY26-end) needs to accelerate materially, not just continue, to hit management's 3,000-store target by March 2027, and a live, recurring ESOP dilution stream (Rs 167 cr in fresh grants in April 2026 alone; ~Rs 419 cr realized by insiders) is a real per-share drag that headline adjusted-EBITDA figures do not capture and that I could not fully quantify from public disclosures.",
        positive="Blinkit's FY26 per-order loss of Rs 3.02 versus Zepto's Rs 78.75 and Swiggy Instamart's Rs 85.18 is a roughly 25-28x unit-economics advantage over its two closest rivals, and it turned segment-adjusted-EBITDA-positive for the first time in Q4FY26 (+Rs 37 cr on Rs 14,386 cr NOV) - the clearest evidence that Blinkit's ~40-46% quick-commerce market share is a genuine, defensible lead rather than a subsidy-bought illusion, especially since Zepto (FY26 net loss Rs 5,905 cr, +26% YoY) and Swiggy (overall net loss widened to Rs 1,092 cr) are both funding their fight with fresh external capital rather than internal cash generation. The 1P inventory-model shift is a completed structural change, not a plan - ~90% of NOV already runs through owned inventory by Q3FY26, contributing over half of a ~1 percentage-point consolidated EBITDA-margin gain, and it was unlocked by a real regulatory milestone (the board's April 2025 cap on foreign ownership at 49.5% to secure Indian-Owned-and-Controlled-Company status under FEMA, approved by over 99% of shareholders). Underneath the quick-commerce story, food delivery remains a stable, moderately-growing (NOV +18.8% YoY), consistently profitable (5.5% EBITDA margin, stable QoQ) duopoly business with Swiggy, and the whole build-out - Rs 2,600 cr invested in Blinkit dark stores and inventory across 2025-early 2026 - has been funded without denting Eternal's cash and investment balance, which held essentially flat around Rs 18,000 cr through the year. Sell-side is broadly constructive rather than cautious (Motilal Oswal Rs 380, Citi Rs 360, consensus ~Rs 345, all meaningfully above the current ~Rs 285-286 price), consistent with a name that has not obviously run ahead of its fundamentals.",
        reverse_dcf="[OPINION] At an EV of roughly Rs 2.6-2.65 lakh crore (mcap ~Rs 2.8-2.85 lakh cr less ~Rs 17,972 cr net cash) against an annualized platform NOV/GOV run-rate of roughly Rs 95,000-1,00,000 cr (Blinkit Rs 14,386 cr and food delivery Rs 9,760 cr Q4FY26 NOV, both x4), EV/run-rate-NOV works out to approximately 2.6-2.7x. That multiple is only clearable if Eternal keeps compounding platform NOV at a blended 30%+ pace for several more years while consolidated EBITDA margin expands from today's bare ~2.4% of adjusted revenue toward something like Meituan's historical mature-market economics (~25% take rate translating to ~4% of GMV as operating profit, i.e. a multi-year margin re-rating, not an incremental one). I judge this achievable but not risk-free: Blinkit's ~25x per-order unit-economics advantage over Zepto and Swiggy Instamart is real, structural, and already visible in the numbers (first EBITDA-positive quarter, cash balance non-eroding despite heavy capex), which argues the market isn't pricing pure fantasy. But Meituan's own 2025 margin collapse under a JD/Alibaba horizontal assault is the closest global precedent for what Amazon Now, Flipkart Minutes, and JioMart could still do to Blinkit's unit economics in India over the next 2-3 years, and that risk is not yet visible in Eternal's numbers because the horizontal giants are still sub-scale here. Net: fair-to-full rather than cheap or absurdly rich - a genuine, clearable growth-and-margin bet, not the kind of story-detached overvaluation that would argue for selling an existing holding.  Growth-estimate detail (moved from expected_next_3y_growth_pct field): [OPINION] ~30 (range 22-40%) blended consolidated revenue CAGR through FY29, heavily front-loaded and decelerating each year - FY27 likely still elevated (40-55%) partly on 1P-reclassification base effects still washing through the YoY comps (not pure demand growth) plus continued store-count ramp toward the 3,000-store March-2027 target; FY28-29 more likely 20-28% as the accounting base effect fully laps and Blinkit NOV growth normalizes from the current 95%+ toward a more mature 25-35% as quick-commerce penetration deepens and competitive intensity (Amazon Now, Flipkart Minutes, JioMart) caps further share gains. This is a real deceleration from the reported 168.6%/196% headline growth rates, which I judge to be substantially inflated by the marketplace-to-1P revenue-recognition shift rather than by underlying volume.",
