        rec='Hold',
        growth_pct=26,
        summary="IDFC First Bank's deposit franchise is a real, ongoing success (CASA 50.8%, retail deposits 79% of the base, up from 27% at merger) but was built at a real cost -- cost-to-income still 72.3% in FY26, versus high-30s/low-40s% for large private peers, and credit-deposit ratio at a tighter 95.5% than most of the sector. The FY25-26 microfinance drag that dominated the narrative is genuinely normalizing: credit cost fell every quarter through FY26 (2.69% to 1.63%) as the MFI book shrank to ~2.3% of loans, and GNPA/NNPA (1.61%/0.48%) are improving, though not yet best-in-class. Q4 FY26's reported PAT of Rs 319 crore masked a messy quarter -- a Rs 483 crore post-tax Chandigarh branch fraud hit (KPMG-confirmed isolated) offset by a Rs 130 crore tax refund, with normalized PAT actually up 145% YoY to Rs 746 crore even as pre-provision operating profit fell 41.6%. The bank's capital position was de-risked via a completed Rs 7,500 crore Warburg Pincus/ADIA preferential raise (CRAR to ~18.9% pro forma), though shareholders notably rejected Warburg's board-seat request. At ~1.4-1.5x book against a mid-single-digit current ROE, the stock is not pricing management's promised mid-teens ROE -- it more closely tracks the Street's own conservative 9-12% recovery case, which makes today's multiple defensible rather than a value trap, but leaves little room for further guidance slippage. Hold.",
        negative_para="The bear case: the deposit franchise has been expensive to build -- cost-to-income is stuck at 72.3%, roughly double what the large private banks run at, and UBS doesn't see it falling much below 67% even by FY27 -- so the operating-leverage half of the ROE bridge is the slower-moving half. Funding is tighter than most peers (CD ratio 95.5%, up from 93.4% YoY), leaving less headroom to keep funding loan growth from deposits alone. Earnings quality needs care: Q4 FY26's headline PAT was a net of a Rs 483 crore post-tax fraud hit and a Rs 130 crore tax credit, and pre-provision operating profit actually fell 41.6% YoY -- a reminder that a branch-level fraud event, even one confirmed isolated by KPMG, is exactly the kind of governance/control-environment flag that recurs as a bank scales branches fast. Most importantly, there is a real, quantifiable gap between management's guided mid-teens ROE and the Street's 9-12% consensus (UBS is at Sell, target Rs 75, below CMP) -- and this bank has a recent history of guided recoveries getting pushed out by exactly this kind of event (MFI stress, then the Chandigarh fraud).",
        positive="The bull case: the deposit franchise keeps getting genuinely better, not just bigger -- CASA at 50.8% and rising, retail deposits at 79% of the base versus 27% at merger, and 32% CAGR deposit growth since 2018, which is a durable, structural asset for a bank of this size. The MFI/credit-cost drag that dominated FY25-26 is on a visible, verifiable downtrend (2.69% to 1.63% credit cost across the four quarters of FY26, MFI book down to 2.3% of loans) rather than a promise still to be delivered, and GNPA/NNPA are improving in the same direction. The balance sheet is now better capitalized than it has been in years: the completed Rs 7,500 crore Warburg Pincus/ADIA infusion lifts pro forma CRAR to ~18.9%, removing a capital-constraint overhang just as loan growth continues at a strong 20%+ YoY clip, and there is no near-term CEO-reappointment binary to add event risk (Vaidyanathan's RBI-approved term runs to Dec-2027).",
        reverse_dcf="[INFERENCE/OPINION] A bank's justified P/B is roughly (ROE - g) / (cost of equity - g). At the bank's current reported ROE of somewhere between 3.8% and 6.5% (source-dependent), this formula is nearly meaningless at any reasonable cost of equity (~13%) and terminal growth (~9-10%) -- it implies close to zero or even negative justified book value on TODAY's earnings power, meaning essentially all of the current ~1.4-1.5x book multiple is a forward bet on the ROE bridge, not a reflection of what the bank earns right now. Working the formula backward from the current 1.4-1.5x multiple (13% cost of equity, 9-10% terminal growth) implies the market is underwriting a sustainable, normalized ROE of roughly 11-12% -- which lines up closely with the Street's own consensus base case (Nomura ~11.8% by FY28, ICICI Direct ~10.8% FY28E, other estimates 10.7-11.2%), not with management's more aggressive mid-teens-to-18% 'core ROE' guidance. That is actually a reasonably comforting finding: the stock is not priced for a fantasy outcome that would make it a value trap if management disappoints again -- it is priced roughly in line with what independent analysts already expect. The asymmetry cuts both ways from here: if management's mid-teens case starts showing up in the numbers (cost-to-income genuinely breaking below 65-67%, credit cost staying on its downtrend, no further one-off shocks), there is real re-rating room toward 1.8-2.0x book; if guidance slips again the way MFI stress and the Chandigarh fraud have twice already, the stock has limited room to de-rate further from a multiple that is already tracking the conservative case rather than the optimistic one. Net: fairly priced against consensus, not against management's own promise -- a genuine, balanced Hold rather than a screaming buy-the-turnaround or a value trap.",
