        rec='Hold',
        growth_pct=10,
        summary="TMPV's India PV+EV business is compounding fast (Q1 FY27 volumes +46% YoY, EV dispatches +112% YoY, Curvv/Harrier/Sierra EV portfolio broadening) but is losing EV category share to Mahindra and JSW MG (Tata's EV share down from 53.4% in FY25 to ~38-39% now) and sits only #3 in overall domestic PV by volume. JLR -- the dominant earnings driver -- is mid-recovery from the Sep-2025 cyberattack (our internal PIT data confirms an actual quarterly net loss of Rs -3,483cr in the Dec-2025 quarter) but the recovery has not yet shown up in volumes: Q1 FY27 wholesales fell 9.2% YoY and China collapsed 26.2%, even as JLR's own FY27 guidance (4% EBIT margin) undershot Street expectations and triggered an 8.27% single-day stock drop in June 2026. Stripping the one-off demerger gain from FY26 PAT, we calculate a clean trailing P/E of ~19.3x against a market cap of Rs 1,23,587cr (price Rs 336, ~25% off the 52-week high) and P/B of 1.10x -- not table-pounding cheap, but not demanding a flawless recovery either. Call: Hold, with a genuine (flagged) Hold-vs-Sell tension given JLR's first post-guidance quarter already missing the recovery narrative.",
        negative_para="JLR -- effectively all of consolidated profit -- has not yet validated its own recovery story: Q1 FY27, the first quarter after the cyberattack-recovery narrative and the June-2026 investor-day guidance, instead showed wholesales down 9.2% YoY, retail down 15.3% YoY, and China down 26.2%/23.9% (wholesale/retail), with management citing a supplier fire and Middle East-conflict disruption on top of the planned Jaguar-lineup phase-out. That guidance itself was a disappointment -- FY27 EBIT margin of just 4% (below the Street's 4.4-6.5% range) on GBP26bn revenue, enough to move the stock down 8.27% in a single session -- and FY26 free cash flow was a GBP2.3bn deficit (versus a GBP1.5bn surplus in FY25), with FY27's target being mere FCF breakeven, not a return to prior profitability. Our own internal PIT data shows this is a real, not cosmetic, hit: an actual quarterly net loss (Rs -3,483cr) in the cyberattack-affected Dec-2025 quarter, and even the 'recovery' Mar-2026 quarter's 11% operating margin remained below the year-ago 15%. US tariff structure adds a portfolio-level asymmetry that persists regardless of demand: UK-built Range Rover/RR Sport get a preferential 10% tariff (capped at 100k units/year under the UK-US deal) while Slovakia-built Defender -- a top-selling nameplate -- faces the full 25% EU tariff, a structural cost disadvantage on one of JLR's best-performing models. On the India side, Tata's EV category leadership is under genuine competitive pressure, not just normalizing off a high base -- share down from 53.4% (FY25) to ~38-39% currently as Mahindra and JSW MG scale up -- and Tata sits only #3 in overall domestic PV volume (13.48% FY26 share) behind both Maruti and Mahindra, well short of its own stated 18-20% ambition.",
        positive="India PV+EV volumes are genuinely accelerating, not just against an easy base: Q1 FY27 dispatches of 182,574 units (+46% YoY) and EV dispatches nearly doubling to 34,467 units, backed by a broadening launch cadence (Curvv EV, Harrier EV, and the newly launched Sierra EV from Rs 18.79 lakh) that gives the company more price points to defend share with. JLR's brand/mix strength held up even through a rough quarter -- Range Rover, Range Rover Sport and Defender together were 80.8% of Q1 FY27 wholesale volume (up from 77.2% a year earlier), meaning the highest-margin, most defensible nameplates are gaining mix share even as overall volume dipped. The balance sheet is not a solvency concern: JLR alone carries GBP2.8bn cash and GBP6.9bn total liquidity (31-Mar-2026), and a costed GBP1.7bn two-year efficiency program targets cash breakeven at 300k units. On our own clean-earnings calculation (stripping the Sep-2025 quarter's one-off demerger gain from FY26 PAT), TMPV trades at roughly 19.3x trailing clean PAT and just 1.10x book value near the lower half of its 52-week range (Rs 336 vs Rs 294-447) -- a valuation that already embeds real caution rather than pricing a flawless turnaround, and brokerage consensus (average target ~Rs 425, CLSA Rs 452, HSBC Rs 450) still implies meaningful upside if JLR merely stabilizes.",
        reverse_dcf="This is a directional judgment, not a formal multi-year FCF/WACC build. Stripping the one-off Sep-2025 demerger gain (Rs 76,248cr recognized in a single quarter against an underlying operating loss that quarter -- confirmed in our own PIT data), TMPV's clean recurring FY26 PAT was roughly Rs 6,398cr, against a Rs 1,23,587cr market cap -- an implied clean trailing P/E near 19.3x, with P/B at just 1.10x. For a business whose dominant earnings engine (JLR) is currently guiding only a 4% EBIT margin -- well below its own historical ~10% aspiration and below what the Street wanted to hear in June 2026 -- a ~19x multiple on trough-adjacent earnings is not obviously cheap on current-year numbers alone; it only looks reasonable if you believe JLR's GBP1.7bn cost-out program and gradual cyberattack/China normalization credibly lift EBIT margin back toward high-single-digits over the next two to three years, which the market (via a consensus target ~26% above CMP, though with real dispersion -- Citi Sell at Rs 320 vs CLSA Outperform at Rs 452) is not uniformly convinced of. Our own read: the discount is neither obviously over-done nor obviously justified -- it is a fair, risk-adjusted price for a franchise with real, proven brand/mix strength (Range Rover/Defender at 80.8% of wholesale) and a real, demonstrated fragility (a single cyberattack cost the company a quarterly loss; Q1 FY27 has not yet shown the guided recovery). The India PV/EV business, growing volumes at 46% YoY and adding launches, is a genuine but currently small offsetting earnings lever that the market is arguably underweighting given JLR's dominance of the headline multiple -- this is the main asymmetry that keeps us at Hold rather than tilting toward Sell.",
