        reverse_dcf="At Rs 756/share (~56x trailing EPS of roughly Rs 13.5-13.8, TTM PAT ~Rs 558cr), the market is pricing Tata Technologies materially above what a simple growth-adjusted comparison to its own peer set supports. Using a rough PEG-style cross-check against our own forward growth estimate (~11%, see below): TATATECH screens at a PEG of roughly 5x, versus LTTS at roughly 2.5x (29x-ish PE against an actual, already-delivered 11.5% YoY growth print with expanding margins) -- TATATECH is priced at roughly double the growth-adjusted multiple of a peer executing at least as well, with a cleaner (non-acquisition-inflated, non-low-base) growth print and no recent single-client profit shock. For TATATECH's 57x to be justified on a reverse basis, the market needs the business to sustain mid-teens-to-20%+ compounding for several years -- essentially betting that Q1 FY27's headline growth (33.8%) is durable and organic, rather than a one-quarter base-effect-plus-acquisition artifact sitting on top of two years of ~flat revenue. We do not think the evidence supports that yet: the organic/inorganic split for Q1 FY27 is undisclosed, the profit-to-revenue growth gap in the same quarter (6.2% vs 33.8%) suggests cost/amortization drag not yet visible in the headline, and the sector's own peers are giving mixed signals (LTTS genuinely accelerating at half the multiple; KPIT guiding an outright decline on European-OEM demand softness). Our judgment: TATATECH is priced for a re-acceleration story that is one quarter old and not yet clean, in a client-concentrated business that has already shown this fiscal year how much downside a single anchor-client shock (JLR cyberattack) can inflict. This is not a distressed-value gap, but it is a rich one -- a re-rating down toward the 30-40x range (where LTTS/KPIT trade, and where TATATECH itself traded for periods pre-FY26) is a plausible multi-quarter path if the FY27 organic growth number, once disclosed cleanly, comes in closer to the historical high-single-digit trend than to the 33.8% headline.",
